 "notes": {
  "disasters": [
   "The winners show the object or the damage itself (a rusted truck, a wreck from the air, a plain hole in sand): one real subject, 0-5 words, often one yellow or white word.",
   "Winners explain the question with a diagram (seat-price grid with 'FRAUD?', 'what changed' before/after) rather than a fire photo; fire/wreck stock art is common among the weak ones too.",
   "Channel brands (e.g. Disaster Breakdown) put the brand across the picture; it did not separate winners from the rest in this sample."
  ],
  "megaprojects": [
   "Winners and weak ones both use big renders and maps; what separates them in this sample is a big concrete number or claim ('$1,000,000,000,000 dream', 'Dear God', 'Can it really work?').",
   "Black label boxes with 2-4 words over a real photo are the rail/transit channels' house style: consistent, readable at small size, mostly typical-to-weak performance.",
   "Aerial or satellite imagery with one marked line (a Roman canal on a map) works as a no-text curiosity thumbnail."
  ],
  "hidden-infrastructure": [
   "Winners are macro or odd-scale objects with little or no text (a glowing washer, gears, an aqueduct flume): curiosity from an unfamiliar image.",
   "Annotated street-level or aerial photos with 1-3 callout labels and an arrow ('No sidewalks?', 'Pole?') carry the how-it-works and city angles.",
   "Weak ones are generic skylines or 'Q&A / how to get involved' pictures with a banner: no single curious object."
  ],
  "mysteries": [
   "Painted ruins, cutaway renders and archival photos with a short curiosity label ('UNDER THE NORTH POLE', 'WHY FAKE THIS?', 'We cracked it') lead.",
   "Pinboard/map collages with red string signal 'investigation' and repeat across channels.",
   "Plain text-less reconstructions of lost buildings are typical, not exceptional."
  ]
 }
}